# Sensi integration

Home Assistant custom integration for Sensi thermostats. Python 3.14+, async
throughout. Shipped code lives in `custom_components/sensi/`.

The full guidance is in @AGENTS.md — read it before a non-trivial change. The
rules below are the ones that most often get broken without it.

## Never emit a credential

No access tokens, refresh tokens, or real `icd_id` values in code, logs,
docstrings, fixtures, or commit messages. `auth.py` exports `redact_token` — use
it for anything token-shaped.

## Parse defensively

The Sensi socket.io protocol is reverse engineered and undocumented; fields
present today can vanish tomorrow, and a `KeyError` in a payload parser takes the
whole integration down. Use `.get()` with a default and the `to_bool` / `to_int`
/ `to_float` helpers from `utils.py`. Never index a payload dict directly.

Do not invent a payload field you cannot find in `tests/sample.json`. There is no
schema to check against — say so instead of guessing.

## Files you must not create or edit

- **`pyproject.toml`** — gitignored on purpose; the devcontainer image supplies
  it. Ruff config goes in `ruff.toml`, pytest config in `pytest.ini`.
- **`manifest.json` `version`** — the release workflow owns it.

`manifest.json` `requirements` must stay in sync with
`requirements_component.txt`; CI fails on drift. `strings.json` cannot contain
literal URLs (hassfest rejects them) — use `description_placeholders`.

## Style

Home Assistant core conventions: docstrings on every module, class and function;
88-column lines; `homeassistant` sorted as first-party with
`force-sort-within-sections`. `ruff check --fix` and `ruff format` handle the
mechanical parts.

## Before calling a change done

```bash
pytest && ruff check . && ruff format --check .
```

New behaviour needs a test; a bug fix needs a test that fails before the fix.
Coverage is gated at 90%. Use `tests/e2e/` when the assertion is about what went
out on the wire.

## Commits

Conventional Commits. `feat:` and `fix:` bump the released version — use them
only for user-visible integration changes. Tooling, CI and docs are `ci:`,
`docs:`, `test:`, `refactor:` or `chore:`.
